\title{Study-Level Random Effects in Continuous-Time Meta-Analysis:\\
  From CoTiMA-FE to CoTiMA-RE}
\author{C. Dormann, T. Hagen, \& $\widehat{\textnormal{C}}$. G. Petee}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

<<setup, include=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  echo = TRUE, eval = FALSE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE,
  purl = FALSE, comment = "#>", tidy = FALSE, size = "small"
)
options(width = 90)
@

\begin{abstract}
Continuous-time meta-analysis (CoTiMA) estimates continuous-time dynamic
models from the participant-level longitudinal data of several primary
studies. In the current study fixed-effects formulation (CoTiMA-FE), study differences
can be represented by $K-1$ time-independent dummy or effect-coded predictors.
This permits every selected model parameter to differ between studies, but it
treats those differences as unrelated fixed coefficients and consequently
does not provide partial pooling or an explicit distribution of study effects.
We describe an extension in which selected drift, diffusion, and initial-state
parameters have study-level random effects. The extension defines study
effects on ctsem's unconstrained parameter scale, before parameter-specific
transformations are applied, and retains a separate level for parameters that
also vary between persons. We then distinguish this joint hierarchical model
from a post-hoc procedure implemented in
\code{CoTiMA\_RE\_moments.R}. The latter reconstructs
study-specific posterior draws from an already fitted CoTiMA-FE model and summarizes
their empirical between-study distribution. Reproducible R workflows for both
approaches are provided.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} continuous-time structural equation model;
individual participant data meta-analysis; multilevel model; partial pooling;
study heterogeneity; ctsem; Stan

\section{Purpose and scope}

The purpose of CoTiMA-RE is to estimate a population
distribution of continuous-time model parameters across primary studies. The
target is study-level heterogeneity, not merely person-level heterogeneity.
For example, the autoregressive drift of a construct may differ between
studies because their populations, measurements, or settings differ. When the
included studies are viewed as a sample from a broader population of studies,
a hierarchical distribution is usually a more appropriate representation than
$K-1$ unrelated study coefficients.

Three levels must be kept conceptually separate:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item repeated observations within persons;
  \item persons within studies, including optional individually varying ctsem
    parameters; and
  \item study-specific deviations from the across-study parameter mean.
\end{enumerate}
CoTiMA-RE is intended to add the third level without removing the second.
Thus, a drift parameter may be fixed across persons but random across studies,
or it may vary at both levels. In the latter case, the study effect shifts the
study-specific mean of the person-level parameter distribution.

\subsection{Terminology: FE, RE, FI, and RI}

The suffixes \emph{FE} and \emph{RE} refer here exclusively to variation
\emph{between studies}. CoTiMA-FE represents study differences by fixed study
indicators, whereas CoTiMA-RE treats study deviations as draws from a
population distribution and partially pools their estimates. This terminology
avoids overloading FI and RI, which are already established in CoTiMA and CLPM
work as abbreviations for \emph{fixed intercept} and \emph{random intercept}.

RI describes variation between individuals in their levels of the variables
or latent processes. RE describes variation between studies in model
parameters, most commonly drift effects. The two distinctions are orthogonal.
For example, \emph{RI CoTiMA-RE} denotes a random-intercept CoTiMA that also
contains study-level random effects. A model with fixed person-level
intercepts can likewise be fitted as CoTiMA-FE or CoTiMA-RE, depending on how
between-study variation is modeled.

\section{Continuous-time model}

Let $s=1,\ldots,K$ index studies, $i=1,\ldots,n_s$ persons, and $t$ continuous
time. A linear stochastic differential equation for a vector of latent
processes $\bm\eta_{is}(t)$ is
\begin{equation}
  d\bm\eta_{is}(t)
  = \left[\bm A_{is}\bm\eta_{is}(t)+\bm b_{is}\right]dt
    + \bm G_{is}\,d\bm W_{is}(t),
  \label{eq:sde}
\end{equation}
where $\bm A$ is the drift matrix, $\bm b$ is the continuous-time intercept,
and $\bm Q=\bm G\bm G^\top$ is the diffusion covariance matrix. A common
measurement model is
\begin{equation}
  \bm y_{is}(t)=\bm\Lambda\bm\eta_{is}(t)+\bm\tau_{is}
    +\bm\varepsilon_{is}(t),
  \qquad \bm\varepsilon_{is}(t)\sim\Normal(\bm0,\bm\Theta).
  \label{eq:measurement}
\end{equation}
The initial state has mean $\bm\mu_{0,is}$ and covariance $\bm P_{0,is}$.
ctsem evaluates the implied discrete-time transition and covariance matrices
for each observed time interval; irregular observation intervals therefore do
not have to be made equal \citep{Driver2017}.

\subsection{Raw and constrained parameter scales}

Many parameters cannot be sampled directly on their reported scale. For a
substantive parameter $p$, let $\eta_{isp}$ denote its unconstrained (raw)
value and let $g_p(\cdot)$ denote its ctsem transformation. The reported value
is
\begin{equation}
  \theta_{isp}=g_p(\eta_{isp}).
  \label{eq:transform}
\end{equation}
Examples include softplus-type transformations for positive variances and
special transformations that preserve stable diagonal drift effects. The
transformation is parameter-specific and is recorded in
\code{ctstanmodelbase\$pars\$transform}. Because $g_p$ is generally nonlinear,
\begin{equation}
  g_p(a+b) \ne g_p(a)+g_p(b).
  \label{eq:nonlinear}
\end{equation}
Predictor and random effects must therefore be added on the raw scale and the
complete result transformed afterwards.

For covariance matrices, ctsem may instead use raw standard deviations and an
unconstrained correlation/Cholesky representation. Random effects on these
components must be applied before the positive-definite covariance matrix is
constructed. Adding an arbitrary random number directly to a variance or
covariance after transformation would not in general preserve positive
definiteness.

\subsection{Time-homogeneous parameters, standardization, and diffusion}

CoTiMA and ordinary ctsem models assume that DRIFT and DIFFUSION are constant
over continuous time within a study. A ctsem regime model can relax this by
using time-dependent predictors. We call the usual assumption
\emph{time-homogeneity of the dynamic parameters}. It must be distinguished
from \emph{covariance stationarity of the stochastic process}.

For a stable drift matrix, the estimated DRIFT matrix $\bm A_s$ and diffusion
covariance $\bm Q_s$ imply a unique asymptotic covariance
$\bm P_{\infty,s}$. These three quantities satisfy the continuous-time
Lyapunov equation
\begin{equation}
  \bm A_s\bm P_{\infty,s}+\bm P_{\infty,s}\bm A_s^\top+\bm Q_s=\bm0.
  \label{eq:lyapunov}
\end{equation}
In a ctsem summary, these correspond to \code{DRIFT},
\code{DIFFUSIONcov}, and \code{asymDIFFUSIONcov}. Thus the numerical check
\begin{verbatim}
A %*% S + S %*% t(A) + Q
\end{verbatim}
should return a matrix close to zero, apart from numerical error. In current
ctsem the usual computational direction is that $\bm A_s$ and $\bm Q_s$ are
estimated and $\bm P_{\infty,s}$ is derived from them. The equality is
therefore an identity and useful consistency check; it does not show that
$\bm Q_s$ was derived from T0VAR.

The stochastic process is covariance-stationary from time zero only when its
initial covariance additionally satisfies
$\bm P_{0,s}=\bm P_{\infty,s}$. Current CoTiMA does not impose this equality.
Only if $\bm P_{\infty,s}$ is independently fixed or otherwise imposed does
Equation~\ref{eq:lyapunov} instead determine the complete diffusion covariance
as
\begin{equation}
  \bm Q_s=-\left(\bm A_s\bm P_{\infty,s}
                  +\bm P_{\infty,s}\bm A_s^\top\right).
  \label{eq:deterministic-diffusion}
\end{equation}
In that special formulation, both diagonal and off-diagonal diffusion
elements are deterministic.

That special constraint is \emph{not} the current CoTiMA formulation. The
time-zero covariance $\bm P_{0,s}$ is estimated freely. An earlier option
constrained it to the asymptotic covariance, but that option is no longer
valid. Consequently, current CoTiMA estimates DRIFT, DIFFUSION, and T0VAR as
distinct parameter sets and derives asymDIFFUSIONcov from DRIFT and
DIFFUSIONcov. Neither the diagonal nor the off-diagonal elements of DIFFUSION
can be calculated from DRIFT and the freely estimated T0VAR matrix.

The CoTiMA-RE interface uses three logical selection matrices to specify
which model parameters are allowed to vary randomly between studies. The
matrix \code{reDRIFT} selects entries of the drift matrix,
\code{reDIFFUSION} selects entries of the process-noise or diffusion matrix,
and \code{reT0VAR} selects entries of the time-zero variance--covariance
matrix. Each selection matrix has the same row and column dimensions as its
corresponding ctsem parameter matrix. A \code{TRUE} entry requests a
study-level random effect for that parameter; a \code{FALSE} entry keeps that
parameter common across studies. We refer to these logical matrices below as
the random-effect selection masks.

Many CoTiMA applications use correlation matrices or variables standardized
within each measurement occasion. Researchers may also combine raw data with
correlation-matrix studies after standardizing the raw variables by occasion.
This data scaling does not itself impose Equation~\ref{eq:lyapunov} and does
not make DIFFUSION deterministic. Therefore, if process-noise variances or
covariances are expected to differ between studies, the corresponding free
lower-triangular entries of the \code{reDIFFUSION} selection matrix may
appropriately be set to \code{TRUE}.
If they are left \code{FALSE}, the model deliberately assumes a common
DIFFUSION parameter across studies; it is not computing study-specific
DIFFUSION values from the study-specific DRIFT effects.

For the time-zero covariance matrix, the \code{reT0VAR} selection should
follow the intended heterogeneity rather than a stationarity identity. In a
univariate analysis of variables standardized to unit variance at time zero,
setting the sole \code{reT0VAR} entry to \code{FALSE} is usually a parsimonious
choice when study variation in that initial variance is not of substantive
interest. The underlying T0 variance remains a freely estimated CoTiMA
parameter unless it was explicitly fixed in the ctsem model. In a
multivariate standardized analysis, diagonal random effects can likewise be
omitted, while free lower-triangular off-diagonal T0 covariance/correlation
elements may be selected to allow initial associations to vary between
studies. With raw-scale data, or when initial variances themselves are expected
to differ, diagonal T0VAR random effects may also be appropriate.

\section{Current CoTiMA-FE}

Suppose $\bm x_s$ contains substantive study-level moderators and
$\bm d_s$ contains $K-1$ dummy or effect-coded study indicators. For parameter
$p$, the current CoTiMA-FE formulation can be written on the raw scale as
\begin{equation}
  \eta_{isp}
   = \alpha_p + \bm x_s^\top\bm\beta_p
     + \bm d_s^\top\bm\gamma_p + v_{isp},
  \label{eq:fi}
\end{equation}
followed by Equation~\ref{eq:transform}. Here $\alpha_p$ is a grand or
reference-study raw parameter, $\bm\beta_p$ contains moderator effects,
$\bm\gamma_p$ contains the $K-1$ study-indicator effects, and $v_{isp}$ is an
optional person-level deviation. When parameter $p$ is not individually
varying, $v_{isp}=0$.

This formulation is flexible: by assigning the indicator effects to drift,
diffusion, and time-zero parameters, every study can obtain its own parameter
value. It is nevertheless a fixed-effects representation of study membership.
It estimates $K-1$ coefficients per affected parameter, imposes no common
heterogeneity distribution, and does not shrink imprecisely estimated studies
towards an across-study mean. The number of coefficients grows linearly with
$K$ for every affected parameter.

The suffix ``FE'' in CoTiMA-FE refers specifically to this fixed
study-indicator formulation. It does not mean that all substantive parameters
are invariant across studies, and it does not describe whether person-level
intercepts are fixed or random.

\section{Hierarchical CoTiMA-RE}

In CoTiMA-RE, the study indicators used solely to reproduce study-specific
parameters are replaced by a study-level latent deviation:
\begin{align}
  \eta_{isp}
    &= \alpha_p + \bm x_s^\top\bm\beta_p + u_{sp}+v_{isp},
    \label{eq:re-linear}\\
  \bm u_s &\sim \Normal(\bm0,\bm\Sigma_{\mathrm{study}}),
    \label{eq:re-distribution}\\
  \bm\Sigma_{\mathrm{study}}
    &=\operatorname{diag}(\bm\tau)\,
      \bm\Omega\,
      \operatorname{diag}(\bm\tau),
    \label{eq:re-covariance-decomposition}\\
  \theta_{isp} &=g_p(\eta_{isp}).
    \label{eq:re-transform}
\end{align}
The vector $\bm u_s$ contains the selected study effects, such as one or more
drift, diffusion, or time-zero parameters. The vector
$\bm\tau=(\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_P)^\top$ contains their marginal between-study
standard deviations, and $\bm\Omega$ is their study-effect correlation
matrix. Hence
\begin{equation}
  \tau_p=\sqrt{\Sigma_{\mathrm{study},pp}},
  \qquad
  \Sigma_{\mathrm{study},pq}=\tau_p\tau_q\Omega_{pq}.
  \label{eq:re-covariance-elements}
\end{equation}
The $\tau_p$ quantify residual between-study heterogeneity after the moderators
in $\bm x_s$ have been included. The off-diagonal elements of $\bm\Omega$ are
correlations between study deviations, while the corresponding off-diagonal
elements of $\bm\Sigma_{\mathrm{study}}$ are covariances. With independent
study effects, $\bm\Omega=\bm I$ and therefore
$u_{sp}\sim\Normal(0,\tau_p^2)$.

If a parameter also varies across persons, a separate distribution is used,
for example
\begin{equation}
  v_{isp}\mid s \sim \Normal(0,\sigma^2_{\mathrm{person},p}).
  \label{eq:person}
\end{equation}
Equations~\ref{eq:re-linear} and \ref{eq:person} imply that $u_{sp}$ shifts the
mean of the person-level distribution in study $s$. Study and person variance
components are distinguishable because all persons from a study share the same
$u_{sp}$, whereas $v_{isp}$ differs between persons. They can nevertheless be
weakly identified when $K$ is small, when studies contain few persons, or when
the same parameter is poorly informed by the observation schedule.

\subsection{Centred and non-centred parameterizations}

The centred parameterization estimates the study deviations directly:
\begin{equation}
  \bm u_s \sim \Normal\!\left(\bm0,
  \operatorname{diag}(\bm\tau)\bm\Omega
  \operatorname{diag}(\bm\tau)\right).
  \label{eq:centred}
\end{equation}
It is often efficient when every study contains enough information to identify
its own dynamic parameters.

The mathematically equivalent non-centred parameterization is
\begin{align}
  \bm z_s &\sim \Normal(\bm0,\bm I),\\
  \bm u_s &= \operatorname{diag}(\bm\tau)\bm L_\Omega\bm z_s,
  \label{eq:noncentred}
\end{align}
where $\bm L_\Omega$ is the Cholesky factor of the study-effect correlation
matrix. With independent study effects, $\bm L_\Omega=\bm I$. The full model
can use an LKJ prior for the correlation matrix and weakly informative positive
priors for $\bm\tau$ \citep{Stan2026}.

The two formulations define the same Bayesian model but can have very
different Hamiltonian Monte Carlo geometry. Non-centred effects are often
preferable for sparse or weakly informed studies; centred effects are often
preferable when each study effect is precisely informed. The argument
\code{parameterization} accepts \code{"centered"}, \code{"noncentered"}, or
\code{"auto"}. The current transparent automatic rule selects the centred
form when the median study contains at least 20 persons and the non-centred
form otherwise. Study size is only a proxy for information, so short diagnostic
fits and HMC diagnostics remain necessary. For joint/conditional ML, unlike
Bayesian inference or marginal ML, the optimized latent-variable mode depends
on the chosen parameterization.

\subsection{Independent, equicorrelated, full, and block covariance structures}

Let $q$ denote the number of selected study-level random parameters. CoTiMA-RE
now supports three global correlation structures. With uncorrelated random
effects (URE),
\begin{equation}
  \bm\Omega=\bm I_q.
\end{equation}
This is selected with \code{covariance = "independent"} (alias
\code{"ure"}). With equicorrelated random effects (ERE), every pairwise
correlation is represented by one common parameter,
\begin{equation}
  \bm\Omega(\rho)=(1-\rho)\bm I_q+\rho\bm J_q,
  \qquad -\frac{1}{q-1}<\rho<1.
  \label{eq:ere}
\end{equation}
This is selected with \code{covariance = "equicorrelated"}; aliases
\code{"equal"} and \code{"ere"} are accepted. Heterogeneity SDs remain
parameter-specific, so equal correlations do not imply equal variances or
equal covariances. With correlated random effects (CRE), an unrestricted
correlation matrix is estimated through its Cholesky factor using
\code{covariance = "full"} (alias \code{"cre"}). URE, ERE, and CRE estimate
zero, one, and $q(q-1)/2$ correlation parameters, respectively. When $q=2$,
ERE and CRE are identical.

Equicorrelation is also known as compound symmetry or exchangeable
correlation. It is a well-established parsimonious covariance structure, but
it can be substantively strong when random effects represent very different
parameters. A common correlation between two DRIFT effects need not plausibly
equal a DRIFT--DIFFUSION or DIFFUSION--T0VAR correlation.

A block structure addresses this limitation:
\begin{equation}
 \bm\Sigma_{\mathrm{study}}=
 \operatorname{blockdiag}(\bm\Sigma_1,\ldots,\bm\Sigma_B).
 \label{eq:block-covariance}
\end{equation}
Each block can independently use URE, ERE, or CRE, while all between-block
correlations are fixed to zero. By default, selected DRIFT, DIFFUSION, and
T0VAR parameters form separate blocks. Advanced users can assign individual
matrix cells to custom blocks. Blocks with one member are automatically
treated as independent; in two-member blocks ERE and CRE estimate the same
single correlation.

For approximately 10 studies, independent effects remain the safest starting
point. A useful next model often uses an equicorrelated DRIFT block and
independent DIFFUSION and T0VAR blocks. A full DRIFT block can be considered
with more studies and a modest number of random drift parameters. A single
global ERE structure is less attractive when it mixes parameter families.
Block independence is itself a restriction: if DRIFT--DIFFUSION heterogeneity
correlations are scientifically important, those effects must be placed in
the same custom block or a future factor structure should be considered.

With roughly 10 studies, estimating many study-effect correlations is usually
ambitious; independent or equicorrelated blocks are safer. With 20--50 studies,
full within-block covariance can be considered when the number of random
parameters is modest and the posterior is well behaved. A factor or sparse
covariance structure is preferable when many dynamic parameters are random.
These are practical guidelines rather than hard thresholds.

\subsection{What constitutes the RE fit?}

The hierarchical model estimates $\alpha_p$, moderator effects, heterogeneity
parameters, and all $\bm u_s$ jointly from the longitudinal likelihood. Its
study-specific estimates are partially pooled posterior estimates. This is the
intended inferential model when studies are treated as draws from a population
of studies. It is not obtained merely by calculating the sample standard
deviation of separately reconstructed study estimates.

The package implementation in \code{ctmaRePrep()} modifies the Stan text
generated by ctsem. It adds a study index, centred or non-centred study effects, their
scale and optional correlation parameters, and study-specific raw parameters
before ctsem applies its internal transformations. The user-facing masks
\code{reDRIFT}, \code{reDIFFUSION}, and \code{reT0var} select the affected
matrix cells.

\subsection{Bayesian, MAP, and ML estimation}

\code{ctmaReFit()} offers three fitting modes. Bayesian estimation samples the full
posterior. Maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimation maximizes the same posterior
density, including the ctsem priors and the study-level scale and correlation
priors. MAP is faster than sampling and can be useful during development, but
a local curvature approximation around the mode is not a substitute for a
skewed or multimodal posterior.

For marginal maximum likelihood, the contribution of study $s$ would be
\begin{equation}
  L_s(\bm\psi,\bm\Sigma_{\mathrm{study}})
  =\int p(\bm y_s\mid\bm u_s,\bm\psi)
    p(\bm u_s\mid\bm\Sigma_{\mathrm{study}})\,d\bm u_s,
  \label{eq:marginal-likelihood}
\end{equation}
and the likelihood is $\prod_s L_s$. This integral can be high-dimensional
because the longitudinal ctsem likelihood is nonlinear in transformed dynamic
parameters. The current implementation does not yet implement adaptive quadrature
or a Laplace approximation to Equation~\ref{eq:marginal-likelihood}.

The available \code{estimation = "ml"} option is therefore explicitly a
\emph{joint/conditional ML} mode. It retains the Gaussian density defining the
study effects, removes ctsem parameter priors and study-level hyperpriors, and
optimizes over the study effects together with the structural parameters. It
is useful as an experimental likelihood-based estimate, but it is not
marginal ML. Heterogeneity may consequently be biased or collapse to the
boundary. A future publication-quality marginal-ML implementation should
integrate the study effects, preferably using a Laplace approximation with
automatic differentiation; Bayesian sampling is currently the safer fully
hierarchical analysis.

\section{Post-hoc moment reconstruction}

The exported \code{ctmaReMoments()} function serves a different purpose. It
starts from an existing CoTiMA-FE fit in which $K-1$ study indicators affect model
parameters. For posterior draw $d$, parameter $p$, and study design row
$\bm d_s$, it reconstructs
\begin{align}
  \eta_{sp}^{(d)}
    &= \alpha_p^{(d)}+\sum_{j=1}^{K-1}d_{sj}\gamma_{pj}^{(d)},
    \label{eq:reconstruct}\\
  \theta_{sp}^{(d)}&=g_p\!\left(\eta_{sp}^{(d)}\right).
    \label{eq:reconstruct-transform}
\end{align}
It then calculates, within every posterior draw, empirical moments over the
$K$ transformed study parameters:
\begin{align}
  \bar\theta_p^{(d)}&=K^{-1}\sum_{s=1}^K\theta_{sp}^{(d)},\\
  \tau_p^{(d)}&=\left\{\frac{1}{K-1}\sum_{s=1}^K
    \left(\theta_{sp}^{(d)}-\bar\theta_p^{(d)}\right)^2\right\}^{1/2},
    \label{eq:empirical-sd}\\
  \Sigma_{pq}^{(d)}&=\frac{1}{K-1}\sum_{s=1}^K
    \left(\theta_{sp}^{(d)}-\bar\theta_p^{(d)}\right)
    \left(\theta_{sq}^{(d)}-\bar\theta_q^{(d)}\right).
\end{align}
Posterior means and credible intervals are subsequently obtained across draws.
This is a posterior, draw-wise empirical method-of-moments summary of the CoTiMA-FE
study parameters. It propagates joint posterior uncertainty from that model,
but it is not full-information maximum likelihood and does not refit a
hierarchical random-effects distribution. In particular, it provides no
partial pooling and can confound sampling uncertainty in study estimates with
genuine heterogeneity. It is useful for auditing, describing, and initializing
a CoTiMA-RE model, but should not be presented as equivalent to the joint
CoTiMA-RE fit.

\subsection{Optional inverse-variance study weighting}

Equal weighting targets the empirical distribution over studies and remains
the default. As a sensitivity analysis, \code{ctmaReMoments()} can calculate a
parameter-specific precision weight from the transformed study draws. Let
\begin{equation}
  \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}_{sp}
  =\operatorname{SD}_{d}\!\left(\theta_{sp}^{(d)}\right),
  \qquad
  w_{sp}=\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}_{sp}^{-2},
  \qquad
  \widetilde w_{sp}=\frac{w_{sp}}{\sum_r w_{rp}}.
\end{equation}
The weighted mean and SD within draw $d$ are
\begin{align}
  \bar\theta_{p,w}^{(d)}
    &=\sum_s\widetilde w_{sp}\theta_{sp}^{(d)},\\
  \tau_{p,w}^{2(d)}
    &=\frac{\sum_s\widetilde w_{sp}
      \left(\theta_{sp}^{(d)}-\bar\theta_{p,w}^{(d)}\right)^2}
      {1-\sum_s\widetilde w_{sp}^{,2}}.
\end{align}
The denominator is algebraically equivalent to
$\sum w-(\sum w^2)/(\sum w)$ before normalization and reduces to the usual
$K-1$ correction under equal weights. The effective number of studies is
\begin{equation}
  K_{\mathrm{eff},p}
  =\frac{(\sum_s w_{sp})^2}{\sum_s w_{sp}^2}
  =\left(\sum_s\widetilde w_{sp}^{,2}\right)^{-1}.
\end{equation}

The SEs are computed after the ctsem transformation. With a Bayesian CoTiMA-FE
fit they are posterior SDs; with an optimization-based fit whose
\code{rawposterior} draws were generated from Hessian-based uncertainty, they
are Hessian/optimization-based SDs. \code{SEsource = "auto"} records the source
from the fit's \code{args\$optimize} flag when available. Explicit
\code{"posterior"} and \code{"hessian"} requests validate that flag and stop
when it contradicts the requested source.

Precision weighting changes the estimand by giving more influence to more
precisely estimated studies. It should therefore normally accompany, rather
than replace, the equal-weight analysis. Furthermore, each parameter has its
own weights, so there is no unique joint weight for cross-parameter covariance
and correlation matrices. Consequently, \code{covarianceDraws} and
\code{correlationDraws} remain equally weighted and the returned
\code{covarianceWeighting} field records that choice.

\subsection{Why the script reads raw posterior columns}

The numerical source is \code{fit\$stanfit\$rawposterior}. The columns labelled
\code{rawpopmeans} and \code{tipredeffectparams} by \code{ctExtract()} are
convenient summaries of parts of the same parameterization, but using their
posterior means would lose nonlinear posterior information. The script maps
each substantive label to its raw-posterior column, combines corresponding
columns draw by draw, and only then applies the parameter transformation.

The main functions are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:functions}.

\noindent\textbf{Important distinction.} The post-hoc workflow is now
available from the package through the exported \path{ctmaReMoments()}
function. It accepts either a CoTiMA full-fit object or its pooled ctsem fit,
uses the raw posterior draws, and returns an object summarized by
\path{summary()}. The lower-level \code{ct\_...} functions in
Table~\ref{tab:functions} remain in \path{CoTiMA_RE_moments.R} as development
and diagnostic utilities. Both interfaces analyze an already fitted
CoTiMA-FE model post hoc and do not fit the hierarchical model.
The hierarchical package interface defines two principal user-facing functions,
\path{ctmaRePrep()} and \path{ctmaReFit()}; hierarchical results are summarized
through the standard \path{summary()} generic. The companion exported
\path{ctmaReMLFit()} function supplies experimental parallel multistart ML.

