\code{rawposterior}. It also preserves the semantic label MANIFESTMEANS when
ctsem internally represents an individually varying manifest mean through an
augmented initial-state parameter.\\
\path{ct_constrain_rawposterior()} & Invokes ctsem's own constraint machinery
for the raw posterior, when constrained ctsem quantities are required.\\
\path{ct_study_design()} & Obtains one row of TI predictor values per study,
or validates a supplied study design matrix.\\
\path{ct_study_parameter_draws()} & Reconstructs raw study parameters and
applies their transformations draw by draw.\\
\path{ct_parameter_matrices()} & Reassembles named parameter draws into
matrix form, including drift, diffusion, T0 means, manifest means, and T0
variance/covariance quantities when represented in the fit.\\
\path{ct_random_effect_summary()} & Calculates study estimates and empirical
between-study means, standard deviations, covariances, and correlations.\\
\path{ct_individual_random_effects()} & Reports person-level variation and
its covariance mapping separately from study-level heterogeneity.\\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}
\endgroup

\section{R workflow: post-hoc summaries from a CoTiMA-FE fit}

For ordinary use, the complete workflow is now:

<<posthoc-package-interface, purl=FALSE>>=
momentFit <- ctmaReMoments(
  CoTiMAFullFit,
  probs = c(.025, .975),
  cores = 4,
  include_matrices = TRUE,
  include_individual = TRUE
)

momentSummary <- summary(momentFit)
momentFit$studyParameters
momentFit$distributionParameters

# Recommended precision-weighted sensitivity analysis:
weightedMomentFit <- ctmaReMoments(
  CoTiMAFullFit,
  weighting = "inverse_variance",
  SEsource = "auto",
  include_matrices = FALSE,
  include_individual = FALSE
)
weightedMomentFit$studySE
weightedMomentFit$studyWeights
weightedMomentFit$effectiveNumberOfStudies
@

The empirical mean and sample SD are calculated across the $K$ reconstructed
study parameters within every posterior draw. Studies receive equal weight,
the variance uses denominator $K-1$, and no hierarchical shrinkage is applied.
The weighted sensitivity analysis uses fixed, parameter-specific inverse-SE-
squared weights estimated across the transformed draws and reports the
effective number of contributing studies.
An explicit \code{study\_design} should be supplied when additional moderators
create more than one TI-predictor row per study.

\subsection{Extracting the ctsem fit}

Both \code{ctmaReMoments()} and \code{ctmaParMap()} accept either the complete
CoTiMA full-fit object or its pooled fitted ctsem object. The package resolves
\code{studyFitList} automatically when it contains one pooled fit. A
\code{ctmaInitFit()} object with $K$ separately fitted studies is ambiguous and
is rejected; in that case, the user must supply one explicitly selected ctsem
fit. The selected fit must contain \code{stanfit\$rawposterior} for moment
estimation. A \code{ctModel} specification without fitted draws is
insufficient.

<<extract-helper, purl=FALSE>>=
cotimaFit <- readRDS("CoTiMA_full_fit.rds")

# Both calls are supported and return the same mapping.
mapFromCoTiMA <- ctmaParMap(cotimaFit)
mapFromCtsem <- ctmaParMap(cotimaFit$studyFitList)
@

The optional \code{est} argument checks dimensions against
\code{ctsem::ctExtract()}, but all mapping and moment calculations use the
absolute columns of \code{stanfit\$rawposterior}:

<<posthoc-load, purl=FALSE>>=
obj <- readRDS("CoTiMA_RE_WEC_f1.rds")

# Optional consistency check against ctExtract output:
est <- ctsem::ctExtract(obj)
map <- ctmaParMap(obj, est = est)
map$rawpopmeans
map$tipredeffectparams
map$individually_varying
map$random_effect_covariance
@

The \code{rawposterior\_col} entries are the columns actually used for the
calculations. The \code{rawpopmeans\_col} and
\code{tipredeffectparams\_col} entries are indices within their respective
blocks, so they need not equal their absolute raw-posterior column numbers.

\subsection{Define or recover the study design}

When all TI predictors are study indicators and there is exactly one unique TI
row per study, the design can usually be recovered automatically. Otherwise,
supplying it explicitly is safer. Its columns must have the same names and use
the same coding as the fitted ctsem model.

<<posthoc-design, purl=FALSE>>=
# Example for eight studies and seven treatment-coded indicators:
Xstudy <- cbind(
  TI1 = c(0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
  TI2 = c(0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
  TI3 = c(0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0),
  TI4 = c(0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0),
  TI5 = c(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0),
  TI6 = c(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0),
  TI7 = c(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1)
)
rownames(Xstudy) <- paste0("Study_", 1:8)
@

For effect coding, the reference row is normally $(-1,\ldots,-1)$ rather than
$(0,\ldots,0)$. The supplied matrix must reproduce the coding used during
fitting; changing it after fitting changes the implied study parameters.

\subsection{Compute and inspect the results}

<<posthoc-estimates, purl=FALSE>>=
momentFit <- ctmaReMoments(
  obj,
  study_design = Xstudy,
  probs = c(.025, .975),
  cores = 4,
  include_matrices = TRUE,
  include_individual = TRUE
)
momentSummary <- summary(momentFit)

# Study-specific transformed posterior summaries:
momentFit$studyParameters

# Empirical between-study moments, summarized across posterior draws:
momentFit$distributionParameters
momentFit$covarianceDraws
momentFit$correlationDraws

# Draws can be retained for custom contrasts or plots:
momentFit$rawStudyDraws
momentFit$transformedStudyDraws

# Reconstructed matrices (including T0VAR/T0COV where available):
momentFit$studyMatrices
momentFit$matrixSummary

# Person-level covariance results, when parameters are individually varying:
momentFit$individualRandomEffects
@

If T0 variance/covariance entries are absent, inspect
\code{map\$random\_effect\_covariance}. In a model with individually varying
initial states, ctsem may encode T0 covariance through the person-level raw SD
and correlation/Cholesky blocks rather than as ordinary raw population means.
The mapping deliberately reports both participating raw-mean columns and the
relevant raw-posterior SD/correlation columns.

The returned object has classes
\code{c("CoTiMAFit", "CoTiMAReMoments")}. Its \code{summary()} method returns
the study estimates, empirical distribution parameters, optional matrix
summary, and optional person-level covariance summary, while printing a brief
reminder that the analysis uses equal study weights and no partial pooling.
For an inverse-variance analysis, the note instead identifies the
parameter-specific precision weighting and its SE source.

\subsection{Moment-function argument reference}

\begin{description}
  \item[\code{fit}] For \code{ctmaReMoments()} and \code{ctmaParMap()}, either
  a CoTiMA full-fit object containing one pooled ctsem fit or the pooled ctsem
  fit itself. A list of separate primary-study fits is rejected as ambiguous.

  \item[\code{est = NULL}] Optional \code{ctsem::ctExtract()} result accepted
  by \code{ctmaParMap()}. It validates the dimensions of
  \code{rawpopmeans} and \code{tipredeffectparams}; the returned absolute
  \code{rawposterior\_col} indices remain the source used by the moment
  calculations.

  \item[\code{study\_design = NULL}] Optional numeric matrix with one row per
  study and one column for every TI predictor, named and coded exactly as in
  \code{ctstanmodelbase\$TIpredNames}. With no additional moderators, unique TI
  rows are normally recovered automatically. An explicit matrix is required
  when moderators create multiple TI profiles within a study or when the
  intended moderator reference values must be chosen deliberately.

  \item[\code{probs = c(.025, .975)}] Lower and upper probabilities for the
  interval summaries. Exactly two increasing values strictly between zero and
  one are required.

  \item[\code{weighting = "equal"}] Weighting used for each parameter's
  empirical mean and between-study SD. \code{"equal"} targets the distribution
  over studies. \code{"inverse\_variance"} uses fixed parameter-specific
  inverse-SE-squared weights estimated from the transformed study draws and is
  recommended as a sensitivity analysis.

  \item[\code{SEweighting = NULL}] Optional logical convenience alias.
  \code{TRUE} overrides \code{weighting} with \code{"inverse\_variance"};
  \code{FALSE} overrides it with \code{"equal"}. The explicit
  \code{weighting} argument is clearer for new code.

  \item[\code{SEsource = "auto"}] Interpretation and validation of the draws
  used to calculate study SEs. \code{"auto"} uses the original fit's
  \code{args\$optimize} flag when available. \code{"posterior"} requires a fit
  that does not report optimization, whereas \code{"hessian"} requires an
  optimization-based fit. All modes calculate the SE as the across-draw SD of
  the transformed study parameter in \code{stanfit\$rawposterior}.

  \item[\code{cores = 2}] Cores passed to \code{ctsem::ctExtract()} only when
  an individually varying parameter summary is requested. The reconstruction
  of study parameters itself is a direct array calculation.

  \item[\code{include\_matrices = TRUE}] Reconstruct DRIFT, DIFFUSION, T0VAR,
  MANIFESTVAR, and other mapped parameter matrices for every study and draw,
  and create \code{matrixSummary}. Setting this to \code{FALSE} reduces memory
  and runtime when only parameter-level moments are needed.

  \item[\code{include\_individual = TRUE}] When parameters also vary between
  people, call the public \code{ctsem::ctExtract()} interface and report their
  covariance, correlation, T0VAR, and T0cov quantities separately from
  between-study heterogeneity. It can be disabled for a faster study-only
  analysis.
\end{description}

The principal return elements are \code{parameterMap}, \code{studyDesign},
\code{rawStudyDraws}, \code{transformedStudyDraws},
\code{studyParameters}, \code{distributionParameters},
\code{studySE}, \code{studyWeights}, \code{effectiveNumberOfStudies},
\code{covarianceDraws}, \code{correlationDraws}, \code{covarianceWeighting}, \code{studyMatrices},
\code{matrixSummary}, and \code{individualRandomEffects}. The
\code{method}, \code{weighting}, and \code{SEsource} fields explicitly
identify the analysis as post-hoc empirical moments and record whether equal
or parameter-specific inverse-variance study weighting was used.

\section{R workflow: specifying and fitting the hierarchical RE model}

\textbf{Required starting point.} The recommended starting point is the
unfitted setup returned by \code{ctmaFit(..., fit = FALSE)}. This call creates
the pooled CoTiMA data and ctsem model specification but does not estimate the
model. The pooled model may represent the $K$ studies with ordinary dummy
coding, scaled dummy coding (\code{scaleTI = TRUE}), or weighted effect coding
(\code{WEC = TRUE}). Separate study fits from \code{ctmaInitFit()} are not a
substitute for this one pooled setup.

<<re-extract-full-fit, purl=FALSE>>=
cotimaSetup <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initialFit,
  fit = FALSE
)
# Supply any remaining substantive model arguments in the call above.
stopifnot(inherits(cotimaSetup, "CoTiMAFit"))

# CoTiMA places the K - 1 study indicators before additional moderators.
K <- length(initialFit$studyList)
studyDummyNames <- cotimaSetup$ctModel$TIpredNames[seq_len(K - 1L)]
@

The explicit \code{studyDummyNames} route is recommended when substantive
moderators are present, because categorical moderators may themselves occupy
several later TI columns and \code{n.moderators} need not equal that column
count. Coding values need not be 0/1: only the $K$ distinct row profiles are
used, so \code{scaleTI = TRUE} and \code{WEC = TRUE} are supported.

\subsection{Store the requested random-effect masks}

With \code{fit = FALSE}, \code{ctmaFit()} returns the data, the ctsem model
specification, and an \code{argumentList}. The latter retains
\code{sameInitialTimes} and \code{CoTiMAStanctArgs}, allowing
\code{ctmaRePrep()} to reproduce relevant ctsem setup options. The user may
define the random-effect masks before preparation. Each mask must be an
$n_{\mathrm{latent}}\times n_{\mathrm{latent}}$ logical matrix. Matrix rows and
columns follow the corresponding ctsem matrix convention.

<<re-masks, purl=FALSE>>=
n.latent <- cotimaSetup$ctModel$n.latent

cotimaSetup$argumentList$randomEffect$reDRIFT <-
  matrix(FALSE, n.latent, n.latent)
cotimaSetup$argumentList$randomEffect$reDIFFUSION <-
  matrix(FALSE, n.latent, n.latent)
cotimaSetup$argumentList$randomEffect$reT0var <-
  matrix(FALSE, n.latent, n.latent)

# Illustrative selections; adapt them to the substantive model.
# Standardization does not make DIFFUSION deterministic in current CoTiMA.
cotimaSetup$argumentList$randomEffect$reDRIFT[1, 1] <- TRUE
cotimaSetup$argumentList$randomEffect$reDIFFUSION[1, 1] <- TRUE
cotimaSetup$argumentList$randomEffect$reT0var[1, 1] <- TRUE
@

The implementation reads \code{reT0var} case-insensitively, so it also accepts
\code{reT0VAR}. A selected cell must be a free parameter. Selecting a fixed
cell, an upper-triangular duplicate, or a model expression that has no raw
population parameter produces an error before Stan is compiled.

\subsection{Prepare and fit}

The data contain a TI variable named \code{study}, constant within each person,
whose values identify studies. It is used as an index and must not also be
estimated as an ordinary linear predictor of the same parameters. Substantive
moderators can remain in the data. By default, \code{ctmaRePrep()} removes the
identified study-indicator effects only for parameters selected as
hierarchical, renumbers the retained TI effects, and leaves later substantive
moderator effects active. This prevents an accidental fixed-plus-random study
hybrid.

If \code{study} is absent, the preparation function attempts to reconstruct
study membership from the study-dummy TI variables. It recognizes ordinary
dummy coding, weighted effect coding, and centred or scaled versions of both.
Detection uses the two- or three-level row patterns rather than requiring the
values 0, 1, or $-1$. Candidate columns must share the same parameter-effect
pattern and $K-1$ columns must produce exactly $K$ distinct TI profiles. A
message reports the columns and number of studies used. If detection is
ambiguous, specify the columns explicitly:

<<re-infer-study, purl=FALSE>>=
prepared <- ctmaRePrep(
  cotimaSetupWithoutStudyColumn,
  study_dummy_ti = c("TI1", "TI2"),
  covariance = "independent"
)
@

This conservative check prevents a binary substantive moderator from being
silently interpreted as part of the study identifier.

When \code{ctmaRePrep()} receives this unfitted \code{CoTiMAFit} object, it
prints a note and calls \code{ctsem::ctFit(..., fit = FALSE)} internally to
generate the necessary Stan code and Stan data. This generates code only; it
does not estimate the intermediate model. The former manual step
\code{ctsemSetup <- ctsem::ctFit(..., fit = FALSE)} is therefore no longer
required. The prepared object retains both representations:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \code{prepared\$CoTiMASetup} is the original object returned by
  \code{ctmaFit(..., fit = FALSE)};
  \item \code{prepared\$ctsemModelSetup} is the complete ctsem setup,
  including the generated Stan representation and \code{ctstanmodelbase}; and
  \item \code{prepared\$original} is the ctsem setup used as the source for
  the study-random-effect Stan patch.
\end{itemize}

\section{Complete hierarchical-function argument reference}

This section documents the package-integrated interface in CoTiMA version
\code{1.0.4}. The live signatures can always be checked with
\code{args(ctmaRePrep)} and \code{args(ctmaReFit)}. Fitted objects are
summarized with the standard \code{summary()} generic.

\subsection{Preparation arguments}

\begin{description}
  \item[\code{model}] Either the unfitted \code{CoTiMAFit} object returned by
  \code{ctmaFit(..., fit = FALSE)}, or an already generated ctsem setup
  containing Stan text, Stan data, setup information, and the base ctsem
  model. In the first case, \code{ctmaRePrep()} generates the ctsem setup
  internally.

  \item[\code{study\_id = NULL}] Optional vector of study labels. Its length
  must equal either the number of ctsem subjects or the number of data rows.
  When supplied at row level, the value must be constant within person. Labels
  need not be consecutive integers; the function internally maps them to
  $1,\ldots,K$ and retains the original labels for reporting.

  \item[\code{study\_id\_ti = NULL}] Name or column number of an existing TI
  predictor containing study membership. If neither \code{study\_id} nor this
  argument is supplied, a TI named \code{study} is used automatically. The
  identifier must not have ordinary TI effects enabled in the ctsem parameter
  table.

  \item[\code{study\_dummy\_ti = NULL}] Optional names or column numbers of the
  $K-1$ study-dummy TI predictors from which membership should be reconstructed
  when \code{study} is absent. If omitted, the function searches TI predictors
  with enabled effects, groups them by their parameter-effect pattern, accepts
  two- or three-level scaled or unscaled coding, and requires $K-1$ columns to
  generate exactly $K$ profiles. Supply this argument when automatic detection
  is ambiguous.

  \item[\code{reDRIFT = NULL}] Logical
  $n_{\mathrm{latent}}\times n_{\mathrm{latent}}$ mask selecting drift cells
  with study-level random effects. \code{NULL} reads
  \code{argumentList\$randomEffect\$reDRIFT}.

  \item[\code{reDIFFUSION = NULL}] Logical matrix selecting diffusion cells.
  \code{NULL} reads the stored \code{reDIFFUSION} mask. Selected cells must be
  free parameters; for symmetric covariance structures, select only the model's
  free/lower-triangular representation. Current CoTiMA estimates DIFFUSION
  separately from the freely estimated T0VAR matrix. Thus both diagonal and
  off-diagonal diffusion cells may have study-level random effects when that
  heterogeneity is intended. Leaving a cell \code{FALSE} constrains that
  diffusion parameter to be common across studies; it does not derive it from
  DRIFT and T0VAR.

  \item[\code{reT0VAR = NULL}] Logical matrix selecting time-zero variance or
  covariance cells. The stored spelling \code{reT0var} is accepted
  case-insensitively. When ctsem has absorbed T0 covariance into its
  individually varying initial-state distribution, diagonal selections act on
  raw SDs and lower-triangular selections act on the unconstrained correlation
  basis. For standardized univariate data, the sole diagonal is often left
  \code{FALSE} as a parsimonious heterogeneity assumption, although the T0
  variance itself remains freely estimated unless explicitly fixed. For
  standardized multivariate data, diagonal random effects may likewise be
  omitted while free lower-triangular off-diagonal cells may be selected to
  model study-varying initial correlations.

  \item[\code{covariance = "independent"}] Study-effect covariance structure.
  \code{"independent"} estimates one heterogeneity SD per selected parameter
  and fixes their correlations to zero. \code{"equicorrelated"} estimates one
  common correlation, \code{"full"} estimates an unrestricted Cholesky
  correlation matrix, and \code{"block"} uses the block definitions below.
  Aliases \code{"ure"}, \code{"ere"}, and \code{"cre"} are accepted;
  \code{"equal"} is also an alias for \code{"equicorrelated"}.

  \item[\code{covarianceBlocks = NULL}] With automatic matrix blocks under
  \code{covariance = "block"}, a named list assigning a structure to every
  selected family. The workflow below illustrates an equicorrelated DRIFT
  block with independent DIFFUSION and T0VAR blocks. Only matrix families
  containing selected random parameters need to be named.

  \item[\code{reBlocks = NULL}] Optional advanced cell-level assignment. Supply
  a named list containing $n_{\mathrm{latent}}\times n_{\mathrm{latent}}$
  character matrices named DRIFT, DIFFUSION, and T0VAR as needed. Every cell
  selected by its RE mask must have exactly one nonmissing block name. A custom
  block may contain parameters from different ctsem matrices.

  \item[\code{blockStructures = NULL}] Named character vector or list assigning
  \code{"independent"}, \code{"equicorrelated"}, or \code{"full"} to every
  custom block in \code{reBlocks}. Between-block correlations are fixed to
  zero. Automatic matrix blocks instead use \code{covarianceBlocks}.

  \item[\code{parameterization = "auto"}] Representation of the latent study
  effects. \code{"centered"} estimates study deviations directly;
  \code{"noncentered"} estimates standard-normal scores and constructs the
  deviations from their SDs and optional correlation factor. \code{"auto"}
  selects centred effects when the median study has at least 20 persons and
  non-centred effects otherwise, and prints the choice and study-size range.

  \item[\code{init = "auto"}] Initialization policy. Automatic initialization
  uses posterior medians from \code{fit\$stanfit\$rawposterior}, with optimized
  raw estimates as fallback. Study values are reconstructed on ctsem's raw
  scale from ordinary dummy, scaled-dummy, or WEC predictors before the fixed
  study effects are removed. \code{"random"} delegates initialization to the
  backend; an expert may instead supply a list or function.

  \item[\code{init\_jitter = 0.02}] Small reproducible raw-scale perturbation
  applied separately to Bayesian chains and parallel optimization starts.

  \item[\code{remove\_study\_fixed\_effects = TRUE}] Remove the identified
  study-indicator effects for parameters selected in the RE masks and renumber
  the remaining TI effects. Later substantive moderators remain active. Set
  this to \code{FALSE} only for a deliberate fixed-plus-random hybrid model.

  \item[\code{sd\_prior = 0.25}] Positive half-normal scale for each raw-scale
  study-effect SD. A scalar is recycled over random parameters; otherwise its
  length must equal the number of selected cells. It is used for Bayesian and
  MAP estimation and disabled in ML mode.

  \item[\code{lkj\_eta = 2}] LKJ shape for full correlation blocks and the LKJ
  kernel restricted to each equicorrelation family. Values above one
  increasingly favor correlations near zero. It is unused by independent
  blocks and disabled in ML mode.

  \item[\code{verbose = TRUE}] Print study-ID reconstruction messages and
  preparation information. Set to \code{FALSE} for silent preparation.
\end{description}

\subsection{Fitting arguments}

\begin{description}
  \item[\code{prepared}] Object returned by
  \code{ctmaRePrep()}.

  \item[\code{estimation = "bayes"}] Estimation target. \code{"bayes"}
  samples the posterior; \code{"map"} optimizes the posterior including ctsem
  priors and study hyperpriors; \code{"ml"} removes these priors and maximizes
  the joint/conditional likelihood. The present ML option does not integrate
  out study effects and is therefore not marginal ML.

  \item[\code{method = NULL}] Backward-compatibility alias. \code{"sample"}
  maps to \code{estimation = "bayes"}; \code{"optimize"} maps to
  \code{"map"}. New code should use \code{estimation}.

  \item[\code{cores = 2}] Number of parallel Bayesian chains that may run.
  A single \code{rstan::optimizing()} call uses one core; use
  \code{ctmaReMLFit()} for parallel independent ML starts. For CRAN examples
  and tests, do not use more than two cores.

  \item[\code{chains = 4}] Number of Markov chains for Bayesian estimation.
  Ignored by MAP and ML.

  \item[\code{iter = 2000}] Total iterations per Bayesian chain, including
  warm-up under rstan defaults, or the maximum optimizer iterations for MAP
  and ML. It can be overridden through \code{fit\_args}.

  \item[\code{seed = 1234}] Random-number seed passed to rstan sampling or
  optimization. Optimizer-specific controls belong in \code{fit\_args}.

  \item[\code{init = NULL}] Optional fitting-stage override. \code{NULL} uses
  the policy stored by \code{ctmaRePrep()}, normally automatic initialization.
  \code{"random"}, a list, or a function overrides it.

  \item[\code{init\_jitter = NULL}] Optional override for the jitter stored in
  the prepared object.

  \item[\code{verbose = TRUE}] Print four high-level stages, elapsed time, and
  rstan optimizer progress. It also controls the backend's native verbosity.

  \item[\code{refresh = 100}] Number of sampling or optimization iterations
  between rstan progress updates. Set to zero to suppress iteration output.

  \item[\code{optimizer\_uncertainty = "none"}] Uncertainty method after MAP
  or ML optimization. The choices are documented separately below. The default
  returns transformed point estimates and avoids a dense joint Hessian.

  \item[\code{compile\_args = list()}] Named arguments passed to
  \code{rstan::stan\_model()}, for example
  \code{list(auto\_write = TRUE)}. These affect compilation, not estimation.

  \item[\code{fit\_args = list()}] Named arguments forwarded to the backend:
  \code{rstan::sampling()} for Bayesian estimation or
  \code{rstan::optimizing()} for MAP/ML. They override wrapper defaults.
  Sampling examples include \code{warmup} and \code{control}; optimizer
  examples include \code{algorithm}, \code{iter}, and tolerance arguments.
  The wrapper manages \code{object}, \code{data}, \code{seed},
  \code{as\_vector}, \code{hessian}, and \code{verbose}.
\end{description}

\subsection{Values of \code{optimizer\_uncertainty}}

These options operate on the complete joint parameter vector, including latent
study effects. Consequently, methods that require a dense covariance matrix
may be unstable or prohibitively expensive in a large model.

In the current package version, only \code{"none"} is enabled for
MAP/ML. All other values are retained in the interface so their intended ctsem
meaning is documented and a targeted error can be returned, but they stop
before compilation. ctsem's post-optimization machinery is not currently
compatible with the custom study-membership data and dynamically compiled
model. Use Bayesian estimation when intervals are required.

\begin{description}
  \item[\code{"none"}] Return the optimized point only. Stan still applies all
  parameter transformations. This is the recommended default for exploratory
  MAP/ML fitting with many study effects. It supplies no interval estimate.

  \item[\code{"hessian"}] Intended to use ctsem's finite-difference Hessian and invert the
  observed curvature. This can fail with NA gradients, boundary heterogeneity
  estimates, weak identification, or hundreds of joint parameters---as in the
  reported ``Non-finite covariance values'' error. It is currently disabled.

  \item[\code{"surrogate"}] Intended to fit a local quadratic surrogate around the optimum
  and use its curvature. By default ctsem evaluates at least
  $\max(4P,50)$ local directions for $P$ parameters, so it can be very costly
  when all study effects are included. It is currently disabled.

  \item[\code{"is"}] Intended for Hessian-based importance sampling followed by uncertainty
  estimation from the weighted importance-sampling distribution. This still
  needs a usable initial Hessian covariance and therefore does not automatically
  solve a Hessian failure. It is currently disabled.

  \item[\code{"bootstrap"}] In ctsem, this is a one-step score bootstrap with
  Hessian ``bread.'' It is currently rejected by the study-RE wrapper because
  ctsem's subject-score subsets do not subset the custom \code{study\_id}
  array. Without that safeguard, Stan reports a data-dimension mismatch.

  \item[\code{"fullbootstrap"}] In ctsem, this resamples independent subjects
  and fully re-optimizes every sample. It is currently rejected for the same
  reason: the resampling code does not carry the custom study-membership array
  into each reconstructed Stan data set.

  \item[\code{"sandwich"}] Robust sandwich covariance using Hessian bread and
  score-covariance meat. It is currently rejected because construction of the
  score matrix uses incompatible one-subject Stan data subsets.

  \item[\code{"opg"}] Outer-product-of-gradients score-information
  approximation. It is currently rejected because it also requires subject
  subsets that omit subsetting \code{study\_id}.
\end{description}

These names remain documented to distinguish possible future uncertainty
methods, but their former ctsem-specific controls are not forwarded to
\code{rstan::optimizing()}. At present, use Bayesian estimation when interval
estimates are required.

\subsection{Summary arguments}

\begin{description}
  \item[\code{object}] Object returned by \code{ctmaReMoments()},
  \code{ctmaReFit()}, or \code{ctmaReMLFit()}. Hierarchical-fit classes include \code{CoTiMAFit} and
  \code{CoTiMAStudyREFit}; the latter lets \code{summary.CoTiMAFit()} route
  the request to the hierarchical RE summary while preserving the established
  summary behavior for ordinary CoTiMA fits.
  \item[\code{probs}] Quantile probabilities; the default is
  \code{c(.025,.5,.975)}. With \path{optimizer_uncertainty = "none"}, only one
  transformed point is available, so all quantile columns equal that estimate.
  A \code{CoTiMAReMoments} object instead retains the two interval
  probabilities supplied to \code{ctmaReMoments()}.
\end{description}

<<re-fit, purl=FALSE>>=
prepared <- ctmaRePrep(
  cotimaSetup,
  study_dummy_ti = studyDummyNames,
  covariance = "independent",  # or "equicorrelated", "full", "block"
  parameterization = "auto",   # or "centered", "noncentered"
  init = "auto",
  init_jitter = 0.02,
  remove_study_fixed_effects = TRUE,
  sd_prior = 0.5,
  lkj_eta = 2
)

# Inspect exactly what will vary before compiling.
prepared$specification[, c(
  "matrix", "row", "col", "parameter", "block",
  "person_varying", "interpretation", "transform"
)]
prepared$covariance_blocks

# A recommended parsimonious matrix-block model:
prepared_block <- ctmaRePrep(
  ctsemFit,
  study_dummy_ti = studyDummyNames,
  covariance = "block",
  covarianceBlocks = list(
    DRIFT = "equicorrelated",
    DIFFUSION = "independent",
    T0VAR = "independent"
  ),
  parameterization = "auto",
  init = "auto"
)

# Advanced example: cell-level blocks for a bivariate model. Only cells whose
# corresponding reDRIFT/reDIFFUSION/reT0VAR mask is TRUE need assignments.
customBlocks <- list(
  DRIFT = matrix(c("auto", "cross", "cross", "auto"), 2, 2,
    byrow = TRUE),
  DIFFUSION = matrix(c("noise", NA, "noise", "noise"), 2, 2,
    byrow = TRUE),
  T0VAR = matrix(c(NA, NA, "initial", NA), 2, 2,
    byrow = TRUE)
)

prepared_custom_block <- ctmaRePrep(
  ctsemFit,
  study_dummy_ti = studyDummyNames,
  covariance = "block",
  reBlocks = customBlocks,
  blockStructures = c(
    auto = "equicorrelated",
    cross = "equicorrelated",
    noise = "full",
    initial = "independent"
  ),
  parameterization = "auto",
  init = "auto"
)

# Full Bayesian posterior sampling:
# Automatic raw-scale values from the pooled CoTiMA-FE fit are jittered by chain.
re_fit <- ctmaReFit(
  prepared,
  estimation = "bayes",
  chains = 4,
  iter = 2000,
  cores = 4,
  verbose = TRUE,
  refresh = 100
)

re_summary <- summary(re_fit)
re_summary

# Maximum a posteriori estimation. This uses ctsem priors plus the
# study-effect SD and correlation priors. Automatic constrained initial values
# are passed directly to rstan::optimizing().
map_fit <- ctmaReFit(
  prepared,
  estimation = "map",
  cores = 1,
  optimizer_uncertainty = "none"
)
map_summary <- summary(map_fit)
map_fit$prior_switches

# Experimental joint/conditional maximum likelihood. This removes parameter
# and hyperpriors, but retains N(0, Sigma_study) as the study-effect model.
# It does NOT integrate out the study effects and is not marginal ML.
ml_fit <- ctmaReFit(
  prepared,
  estimation = "ml",
  cores = 1,
  optimizer_uncertainty = "none"
)
ml_summary <- summary(ml_fit)
ml_fit$prior_switches

@

The resulting hierarchical fits have classes
\code{c("CoTiMAFit", "CoTiMAStudyREFit", "ctmaStudyREFit")}. Consequently,
users call \code{summary(re\_fit)} rather than a separate RE-summary function.
The package's \code{summary.CoTiMAFit()} method checks for the
\code{CoTiMAStudyREFit} subclass and delegates to the internal RE summary;
it also checks for \code{CoTiMAReMoments} and delegates to the internal moment
summary. Moment objects report equal-weight empirical study moments, whereas
hierarchical fit objects report population, heterogeneity, correlation, and
partially pooled study estimates. In both cases,
ordinary CoTiMA fits continue to print their stored \code{summary} element.
The older snake-case function names remain aliases for compatibility but are
not used in new code.

The returned hierarchical summary contains both
\code{study\_sd\_raw} and \code{study\_sd\_transformed}. The former reports
$\tau_p$ on ctsem's unconstrained estimation scale. The latter reports the
model-implied standard deviation of the complete transformed parameter
distribution $g_p(\alpha_p+u_{sp})$, with
$u_{sp}\sim\mathcal N(0,\tau_p^2)$. It is calculated separately for every
posterior draw using numerical Gaussian quadrature and then summarized across
draws. This is important because, for a nonlinear $g_p$,
\code{g(tau)} is not a standard deviation. The transformed table is available
for parameters having a scalar ctsem transformation, such as DRIFT and
DIFFUSION parameters. T0 covariance components are omitted from that table
because their final scale depends jointly on the complete Cholesky matrix;
their study-specific final values remain in \code{study\_T0VAR} and
\code{study\_T0cov}.

Calling \code{summary()} prints a short interpretation note because two
population estimands must not be confused. The backward-compatible
\code{population\_parameters} table and its explicit alias
\code{population\_parameters\_raw\_centre} report
$g_p(\alpha_p)$: the population centre is defined on ctsem's raw estimation
scale and is then transformed. With nonlinear $g_p$, this is generally not the
mean natural-scale study parameter. The table
\code{population\_parameters\_study\_average} instead computes
$K^{-1}\sum_s g_p(\alpha_p+u_{sp})$ within every posterior draw and then
summarizes those draw-wise equal-study averages. This is normally the relevant
meta-analytic population estimand. The same equal-study principle is used for
\code{population\_T0VAR\_study\_average} and
\code{population\_T0cov\_study\_average}; these use the complete jointly
transformed study matrices rather than transforming individual raw Cholesky
elements. None of the heterogeneity SD tables is a standard error of the
population mean.

<<re-summary-output, eval=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
re_summary <- summary(re_fit)
re_summary$estimand_interpretation
re_summary$population_parameters_raw_centre
re_summary$population_parameters_study_average
re_summary$study_sd_raw
re_summary$study_sd_transformed
@

\subsection{Experimental parallel multistart joint ML}

The exported \code{ctmaReMLFit()} function avoids ctsem's private optimizer and
calls \code{rstan::optimizing()} directly. One
Stan optimization is not multithreaded. Instead, \code{ctmaReMLFit()} compiles
the modified model once, launches independent starting values on forked
macOS/Linux worker processes, rejects failed or non-finite solutions, and
retains the solution with the largest joint objective. This uses multiple
cores to search for local modes; it does not turn the estimator into marginal
ML and does not make its objective invariant to centred versus non-centred
latent coordinates.

<<re-parallel-ml, purl=FALSE>>=
parallel_ml_fit <- ctmaReMLFit(
  prepared,
  parameterization = "centered",
  cores = 4,
  n_starts = 8,
  init = "auto",
  init_jitter = 0.02,
  seed = 1234,
  algorithm = "LBFGS",
  iter = 2000,
  refresh = 100
)
parallel_ml_summary <- summary(parallel_ml_fit)

# Compare convergence and objective values across starts.
vapply(parallel_ml_fit$attempts, `[[`, numeric(1), "value")
lapply(parallel_ml_fit$attempts,
  function(x) x[c("ok", "error", "warnings")])
@

The principal arguments are \code{cores}, the maximum concurrent workers;
\code{n\_starts}, the total independent optimizations; \code{init}, a common
initialization specification or function (\code{"auto"} uses independently
jittered copies of the FI-based template); \code{algorithm}, \code{iter}, and
\code{refresh}, passed to RStan optimization; \code{compile\_args} and
\code{optim\_args}, for additional named backend controls; and
\code{poll\_seconds}, which controls how often the parent process checks for
completed starts. The function currently stops on Windows because compiled
RStan model pointers cannot safely be sent to ordinary PSOCK workers without
independent worker setup or recompilation.

With \code{verbose = TRUE} (the default), the wrapper reports four stages:
Stan compilation, fitting, extraction of results, and completion, together
with elapsed time. Informational text uses the automatically wrapped
81-character message boxes customary in CoTiMA. Genuine warnings and errors
remain R conditions so calling code can detect them. During Bayesian estimation, \code{refresh} controls how
often Stan prints iteration progress. During MAP and ML optimization, the
wrapper enables rstan's optimizer messages. Use \code{verbose = FALSE}
and \code{refresh = 0} for silent execution. Compilation and optimization do
not expose a reliable percentage-complete value, so the stage messages avoid
displaying a misleading progress bar.

By default, MAP and ML use \code{optimizer\_uncertainty = "none"}. This returns
transformed point estimates without attempting a dense Hessian over all study
effects. The summary's lower, median, and upper columns then equal the point
estimate. Optimization-based uncertainty can be re-enabled only in a future
implementation that correctly carries study membership
through study-aware transformed-sample and subject-subsetting machinery. The
current implementation rejects all non-\code{"none"} values before fitting.
Profile likelihood, a study-aware parametric bootstrap, or a correctly
implemented marginal-information matrix would be preferable for
likelihood-based intervals; Bayesian sampling is presently implemented.

MAP and conditional ML now call the exported \code{rstan::optimizing()}
interface directly; they no longer retrieve ctsem's unexported
\code{stanoptimis()} function. MAP sets both ctsem's ordinary prior switch and
the study-hyperprior switch to one. Conditional ML sets both switches to zero,
while retaining the Gaussian study-effect density that defines the random
effects. The optimizer returns constrained parameters and transformed
parameters directly, which are passed to the common CoTiMA-RE summary.

A single \code{rstan::optimizing()} run uses one core. Bayesian sampling can
still run chains in parallel, and \code{ctmaReMLFit()} uses several cores for
independent ML starts. Preparation retains
\code{standata\$recompile = 1} as a marker that the generated Stan program was
modified and is not ctsem's original cached model.

Versions before \code{2026-08-10.17} incorrectly interpreted ctsem's
\code{whenvecp} coding. ctsem selects entries that are \emph{not} zero; setting
a selected raw parameter to zero therefore disabled its subject-level
transformation. The corrected code stores the raw parameter's own index in
\code{whenvecp}, activates the second \code{matsetup} gate, refreshes the
selected system matrices and JAx, and rejects stale prepared objects in the
parallel optimizer. A deterministic one-evaluation check confirmed that forced
study effects then produce distinct subject DRIFT matrices. After refitting,
exact equality can still occur legitimately when a heterogeneity SD is
estimated at zero; inspect
\code{results\$study\_sd\_raw} and
\code{results\$study\_effects\_raw} to distinguish that boundary solution from
the former scheduling error.

If the exact argument names of the local ctsem backend differ, inspect
\path{args(ctmaReFit)} and the generated \code{prepared} object.
The preparation step is deterministic and can be tested without running an
expensive model.

\subsection{Person-level and study-level variation in the same parameter}

No special mask is required for the combination. The existing
\code{indvarying} setting in \code{ctstanmodelbase\$pars} determines whether a
parameter has person-level variation; the corresponding \code{re...} mask
adds study-level variation. Before fitting, inspect the generated
specification:

<<two-level-check, purl=FALSE>>=
spec <- prepared$specification

subset(spec, person_varying)[, c(
  "parameter", "interpretation", "transform"
)]
subset(spec, !person_varying)[, c(
  "parameter", "interpretation", "transform"
)]
@

For a person-varying drift or diffusion parameter, the study effect shifts the
mean of that person's raw parameter distribution. If T0 variation has been
absorbed into ctsem's individual initial-state covariance, the implementation maps
a selected T0 diagonal to its raw SD and a lower-triangular off-diagonal to its
unconstrained correlation basis. Warnings and errors should be treated as
model-specification diagnostics, not suppressed automatically.

CoTiMA's alternative random-intercept construction uses one zero-drift pseudo
process per substantive latent. These processes can be recognized by T0-mean
parameter names such as \code{MeanV1\_cint}. Their freely estimated T0
(co)variances describe the distribution of person-level random intercepts,
although ctsem need not mark the corresponding T0VAR row itself as
\code{indvarying}. Consequently, \code{ctmaRePrep()} recognizes this naming
pattern. A selected pseudo-process diagonal is labelled as a study-varying
variance of a person-level random intercept; a selected off-diagonal is labelled
as a study-varying random-intercept covariance component. This classification
changes the reported interpretation, not the Stan parameter mapping: the study
effect already acts on the appropriate freely estimated T0 (co)variance.

\section{Diagnostics and reporting}

Centred and non-centred parameterizations should be compared through short
diagnostic runs before committing to long sampling. The central HMC quantities
are divergences, the proportion of iterations that reach
\code{max\_treedepth}, step size, leapfrog count, energy/BFMI, effective sample
size, and between-chain $\widehat R$. A one-chain test cannot establish
convergence, and a 50-draw test cannot support substantive inference. If most
iterations reach maximum tree depth, merely increasing the limit can multiply
runtime without correcting posterior geometry. With a modest parameter count
but strong posterior correlations, a longer adaptation phase and
\code{metric = "dense\_e"} can be tested through
\code{fit\_args = list(warmup = ..., control = list(metric = "dense\_e"))}.
Persistent saturation under both parameterizations indicates that structural
reparameterization or scaling, rather than a longer production run, should be
investigated.

An article reporting CoTiMA-RE should state:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item which model matrices and cells had study-level random effects;
  \item whether those parameters also varied between persons;
  \item the raw-scale transformations used by ctsem;
  \item the covariance structure of $\bm u_s$ and all priors;
  \item the number of studies, persons per study, and observation schedules;
  \item posterior means and uncertainty intervals for population parameters,
    heterogeneity SDs, correlations, and study-specific deviations;
  \item convergence diagnostics, effective sample sizes, divergences, and
    posterior predictive checks; and
  \item sensitivity analyses for heterogeneity priors and covariance structure.
\end{enumerate}

Heterogeneity on the raw scale is computationally fundamental but may not be
substantively intuitive. Report transformed study-specific parameters and
derived dynamic quantities as well. The \code{study\_sd\_transformed} summary
component gives the model-implied transformed-scale SD for scalar transformed
parameters. Because nonlinear transformations make the transformed
distribution asymmetric, this quantity is computed from the transformed
distribution for every posterior draw, rather than by transforming a raw SD
or only transforming posterior means.

\section{Integration into the CoTiMA package}

The implementation is now integrated into ordinary package files. The shared
preparation, Stan-patching, fitting, and summary helpers, together with the
exported \code{ctmaRePrep()} and \code{ctmaReFit()}, are stored in
\path{R/ctmaReFit.R}. The exported parallel multistart optimizer
\code{ctmaReMLFit()} is stored in \path{R/ctmaReMLFit.R}. The established
moment interface \code{ctmaReMoments()} and its internal reconstruction and
summary helpers are stored in \path{R/ctmaReMoments.R}; the exported mapping
function \code{ctmaParMap()} and its object-resolution helpers are stored in
\path{R/ctmaParMap.R}. The established
\code{summary.CoTiMAFit()} method remains defined only in
\path{R/ctmaSummary.R}; it routes objects inheriting from
\code{CoTiMAStudyREFit} to the internal \code{.ctmaReSummary()}. The top-level
\code{CoTiMAReMoments} objects are routed to
\code{.ctmaReMomentsSummary()}. The top-level
scripts with corresponding names are development loaders and are excluded
from the source package, so they do not duplicate namespace definitions.

Roxygen documentation exports \code{ctmaRePrep()}, \code{ctmaReFit()},
\code{ctmaReMLFit()}, \code{ctmaReMoments()}, and \code{ctmaParMap()}, together
with the existing S3 summary method. All dot-prefixed helpers remain internal. Because
the implementation calls \code{rstan::} functions directly, \code{rstan} must
be declared in \code{DESCRIPTION}; \code{Suggests} is appropriate if
CoTiMA-RE is optional and every entry point checks
\code{requireNamespace("rstan", quietly=TRUE)}, whereas \code{Imports} is
appropriate if it becomes mandatory package functionality. Long model fits
must not run in CRAN examples or routine tests.

Package code now uses public rstan interfaces for Bayesian sampling, MAP,
single-start conditional ML, and parallel multistart conditional ML. It no
longer depends on ctsem's unexported \code{stanoptimis()} implementation. This
removes a fragile dependency on ctsem internals and is safer for CRAN
submission.

The generated Stan patch depends on identifiable insertion anchors in ctsem's
model text. The package therefore retains explicit anchor-count errors and
records the ctsem version in every prepared object. Fast package tests cover
covariance aliases, automatic and custom block construction, invalid selected
cells, ML/MAP prior switches, study-effect draw dimensions, CoTiMA/ctsem
parameter-map input routes, raw-posterior column mapping, and equal-weight
moment calculations. Further tests
should be added for each supported ctsem release, dummy/WEC/scaled-study
reconstruction, parameter ordering, initialization dimensions, and Stan syntax
generation. One small optional integration test can fit a very short model
when rstan is available. Saved compiled Stan models and platform-specific
binaries are excluded from the source package.

Study-effect variances are difficult to estimate with very small $K$. With
$K=3$, a hierarchical model can be fitted only under strong regularization and
its heterogeneity posterior will be heavily prior-sensitive. For the anticipated
range $K=10$--$50$, begin with a small, theory-driven set of random parameters
and independent study effects. Add correlations only when the number of
studies and the likelihood provide adequate information.

\section{Recommended interpretation}

The package functions support two related but different questions. \code{ctmaReMoments()}
asks: ``What empirical distribution is implied by the study-specific fixed
effects in this already fitted CoTiMA-FE model?'' The hierarchical
\code{ctmaRePrep()}--\code{ctmaReFit()} workflow asks: ``What
population distribution of study effects is supported when study heterogeneity
is estimated jointly with the longitudinal model?'' The first is valuable for
verification and exploration. The hierarchical workflow is the appropriate primary analysis
when the inferential aim is generalization to a population of studies.
\code{ctmaReMLFit()} is an experimental multistart implementation of the same
joint/conditional ML objective and is primarily useful for optimization
diagnostics; it is not a marginal-ML replacement for the Bayesian model.

\section*{Reproducibility notes}

The following commands knit this document in RStudio or from the R console.

<<knit-document, purl=FALSE>>=
knitr::knit2pdf(
  "CoTiMA_Random_Effects.Rnw",
  clean = FALSE
)

# Record package versions used for an analysis:
sessionInfo()
citation("CoTiMA")
citation("ctsem")
@

The source requires repeated LaTeX passes because citations, equation
references, table references, and PDF bookmarks are recorded in auxiliary
files during the first pass. The RStudio directive at the top selects knitr.
If a local RStudio configuration nevertheless runs only one \code{pdflatex}
pass, use the \code{knit2pdf()} command above. Keeping \code{clean = FALSE}
retains the \code{.tex} and \code{.log} files for troubleshooting.

All example chunks are set to \code{eval = FALSE} so that the manuscript can
be knitted without private fit files or a Stan toolchain. For a fully
reproducible supplement, change selected chunks to \code{eval = TRUE}, use
project-relative paths, and archive the fitted object together with the exact
CoTiMA, ctsem, Stan, and R versions.

